## 1. Summary for a busy reader

**The question.** A beer brand can give each of its {{ n.n_outlets | vnd }} outlets one trade action per month:
nothing, a messaging-app reminder, a price promotion, a display kit or a sales rep visit. Which outlet
should get which action, given the costs and the monthly limits on display kits and rep visits?

**The answer.** Choosing the action per outlet with a model of each action's effect earns more than the
current business rule (BAU), as long as the limits allow it. On {{ n.n_test_outlets | vnd }} outlets the
model never saw:

* **Model, no limits** (best action per outlet-month): estimated net gain over doing nothing
  **{{ best.est_gain | vnd }} VND per outlet-month** (95% confidence interval {{ best.ci_low | vnd }} to
  {{ best.ci_high | vnd }}). The true gain, known because the data is synthetic, is {{ best.true_gain | vnd }} VND.
* **BAU rule**: estimated {{ bau.est_gain | vnd }} VND (true {{ bau.true_gain | vnd }} VND).
* **Model minus BAU**, measured on the same outlets: **{{ pb.est_difference | vnd }} VND per outlet-month**
  (CI {{ pb.ci_low | vnd }} to {{ pb.ci_high | vnd }}); true difference {{ pb.true_difference | vnd }} VND.
  For {{ n.n_outlets | vnd }} outlets that is about {{ (pb.est_difference * n.n_outlets) | mvnd }} million VND
  of extra net margin per month.{% if pb.ci_low <= 0 %} The interval includes zero, so these 6,000 test outlets
  alone do not prove the difference. A second measurement on the same outlets (overfit report, Part 12) gives
  {{ tval("overfit", "test", metric="gain_vs_bau_rule") | vnd }} VND with an interval above zero, but it is scored
  with the trained model's own outcome models, so it is not an independent check. This report uses the referee's
  number as the headline because the referee never grades the model with its own predictions. `model_best`
  spends more on arms than BAU (about {{ spend("model_best") | float | vnd }} against {{ spend("bau_rule") | float | vnd }} VND per
  outlet-month), so its gain is net of higher spending.{% endif %}
* **With the limits.** BAU breaks the limits: it gives display kits to {{ bau["%_display"] | f(1) }}% and rep
  visits to {{ bau["%_rep"] | f(1) }}% of outlet-months, against caps of about 6% and 8%. Cut down to the caps
  (at random), BAU is worth {{ baucap.est_gain | vnd }} VND (true {{ baucap.true_gain | vnd }}). The model within
  the caps is worth {{ capp.est_gain | vnd }} VND (true {{ capp.true_gain | vnd }}). In truth the model is
  {{ pcl.true_difference | vnd }} VND per outlet-month better, but the estimate, which is all a real project would
  have, is **{{ pcl.est_difference | vnd }} VND (CI {{ pcl.ci_low | vnd }} to {{ pcl.ci_high | vnd }}): it cannot tell
  the two apart** on {{ n.n_test_outlets | vnd }} outlets.
* **Ceiling** (true best action everywhere): {{ orc.true_gain | vnd }} VND. The model without limits reaches
  {{ (best.true_gain / orc.true_gain) | pct(0) }} of it; BAU reaches {{ (bau.true_gain / orc.true_gain) | pct(0) }}.

**Main weakness.** The model gets the size of the price promo effect too flat: it predicts that the promo pays
for itself almost everywhere, while in truth it loses money where the brand already has most of the outlet's
volume. Under the caps this matters, because the promo then fills most of the rows that cannot get a display
kit or a rep visit (section 7, Parts 7 and 11).

**Recommendation.** The model ranks outlets for display kits and rep visits better than random, and the caps
cost money (section 7). Within the caps, its advantage over BAU is real in the simulation but not shown by the
data. So: run the model within the caps as a randomised pilot against BAU within the caps, with no price promo
where the brand share is above 0.6, and keep about 10% of outlets on a random arm to keep measuring. Section 9
sizes the pilot: all outlets outside the 10% holdout (about 9,000 per side), 3 months and the adjusted analysis, the smallest difference it detects
with 80% power is {{ tval("pilot_power", "mde_80_power", months=3, analysis="adjusted") | vnd }} VND per outlet-month,
larger than the true gap. At the true gap of the policy it would test ({{ pgl.true_difference | vnd }}) its power is
about {{ (100 * phi(pgl.true_difference / se3 - 1.96)) | round | int }}%, so a "no clear difference" result is quite
possible even though the model is better in the simulation.

## 2. Business context

**Who decides, what, how often.** The trade marketing team of a beer brand decides once a month, for each of
its {{ n.n_outlets | vnd }} retail outlets, which single trade action the outlet gets that month. The same
outlet can get a rep visit in March and nothing in April. Outlets are bars, restaurants, groceries,
minimarts and modern trade stores (supermarket chains) in five regions.

**The five arms.** Each possible action is called an *arm*. Costs are per outlet per month and illustrative.

| Code | Arm | What it physically is | Cost (VND) | Limited per region and month? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
{%- for k in ["0", "1", "2", "3", "4"] %}
| {{ k }} | `{{ n.arm_labels[k] }}` | {{ n.arm_descriptions[k] }} | {{ n.cost[k] | vnd }} | {{ "yes" if k in n.capacity_full else "no" }} |
{%- endfor %}

Each extra case ordered is worth {{ n.value_per_outcome | vnd }} VND of gross margin (`value_per_outcome`). The
*net value* of an arm for one outlet-month is `{{ n.value_per_outcome | vnd }} x (extra cases) - cost`. So a
rep visit pays for itself above {{ (n.cost["4"] / n.value_per_outcome) | f(1) }} extra cases, a display kit
above {{ (n.cost["3"] / n.value_per_outcome) | f(1) }}, a price promo above
{{ (n.cost["2"] / n.value_per_outcome) | f(1) }} and a message above {{ (n.cost["1"] / n.value_per_outcome) | f(2) }}.

The promo cost is a fixed average per outlet. A real "buy 10 get 1" costs more where more is ordered; the
method takes one fixed cost per arm, so this is a simplification of the demo.

{{ table("capacity_full", rename={"region": "region", "outlets": "outlets in region"}) }}

Display kits are limited to about 6% and rep visits to about 8% of the outlets of a region per month.

**Why "the best arm per outlet" is not obvious.**

* *Costs differ by a factor of 200*, so the arm with the largest effect is not the arm with the best net value.
* *Capacity*: reps and display kits cannot reach every outlet that would benefit.
* *Effects differ by outlet*: a rep visit works in a large bar with falling sales and does little in a
  small grocery with steady sales; a display kit works in a grocery with coolers and not in a bar.
* *Some actions lose money*: a price promo where the brand already has most of the outlet's beer volume
  mostly gives away cases that would have been bought anyway.
* *The data is not a clean experiment*: 30% of past actions followed the BAU rule, so comparing outlets
  that got an action with outlets that did not is biased (section 3).

