### Part 1. Data

**Purpose.** Describe the panel: one row is one outlet in one month.

**What it is.** {{ n.n_rows | vnd }} rows = {{ n.n_outlets | vnd }} outlets x {{ n.n_months }} months, from
{{ n.month_first }} to {{ n.month_last }}. {{ n.tet_month }} is the month of the lunar new year (Tet), when beer sales
peak; May and June start the rainy season. The outcome is `cases_ordered`: cases of beer the outlet ordered
in the month, 0 if it did not order (unit: cases per outlet-month). The mean is {{ n.mean_cases | f(1) }} cases;
{{ n.zero_share | pct }} of outlet-months have no order; when an outlet orders, the median is
{{ n.median_cases_if_order | f(1) }} cases.

**How arms were assigned.** {{ n.randomised_share | pct }} of outlet-months were in a randomised *trade test*:
the arm was drawn at random among the arms allowed for the outlet. Modern trade stores could only get
`control`, `zalo_message` or `price_promo` (no display kits or rep visits in supermarket chains). The other
outlet-months followed the BAU rule, the first matching line of:

| # | Condition | BAU arm |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | modern trade and `brand_share_est` below 0.4 | `price_promo` |
| 2 | modern trade (other) | `control` |
| 3 | on-trade (bar, restaurant) and size large | `rep_visit` |
| 4 | on-trade, size medium and `vol_trend_3m` below -0.15 | `rep_visit` |
| 5 | off-trade (grocery, minimart) with at least 2 coolers | `display_kit` |
| 6 | `vol_trend_3m` below -0.2 | `price_promo` |
| 7 | `days_since_last_order` above 45 | `zalo_message` |
| 8 | otherwise | `control` |

The rule's arm is stored in `rule_arm` for every row, so the BAU rule can be valued as a policy. It ignores
the capacity limits: in the simulated history the caps were not enforced. The caps of section 2 are a planning
constraint for the coming months, which is why every capped policy is compared with BAU cut down to the same caps.

