## 4. Ground truth

{{ nbref(2) }}

### Part 2. Ground truth (synthetic data only)

**Purpose.** State the true effect structure the generator used, so the reader can judge the model against
it. Real projects do not have this; it exists here only because the data is simulated.

**The true effects, in words.** Each arm changes the chance that the outlet orders this month and/or the
number of cases it orders. Every arm's effect on order probability and on cases per order is multiplied by 1.4 in the Tet month.

| Arm | Works best for | Little or negative effect for |
|---|---|---|
| `zalo_message` | Outlets that have not ordered for a long time (above about 40 days) | Regular buyers: close to zero |
| `price_promo` | Outlets where our brand share is low, more when a competitor runs a promo; larger outlets | Outlets where our share is high: the promo pays for cases that would be bought anyway |
| `display_kit` | Groceries and minimarts with coolers, more coolers and larger outlets | Bars and restaurants; outlets without coolers |
| `rep_visit` | Large bars and restaurants whose sales are falling (size x trend interaction) | Modern trade, where store staff cannot order |

{{ table("true_effects_by_channel", rename={"tau_zalo_message": "tau z", "tau_price_promo": "tau p",
  "tau_display_kit": "tau d", "tau_rep_visit": "tau r", "net_zalo_message": "net z", "net_price_promo": "net p",
  "net_display_kit": "net d", "net_rep_visit": "net r"}) }}

**How to read it.** z, p, d and r stand for `zalo_message`, `price_promo`, `display_kit` and `rep_visit`. `tau`
columns are the true average extra cases per outlet-month; `net` columns are
{{ n.value_per_outcome | vnd }} x tau minus the cost, in VND per outlet-month. A negative net value means the arm
loses money on average in that channel.

{{ table("true_best_by_channel") }}

The oracle may pick an arm a channel never got: in modern trade the true best arm is a rep visit for a few rows,
although rep visits are never given there. The oracle is a ceiling, not an available policy.

{{ table("promo_by_share_truth", rename={"share_rows_promo_loses_%": "% rows where promo loses money"}) }}

**Finding.** The true best arm depends on the channel: `rep_visit` in bars and restaurants, `display_kit` or
`rep_visit` in groceries and minimarts, `price_promo` in modern trade. The price promo loses money on
average once `brand_share_est` is above about 0.6 to 0.7. Every arm loses money for some rows: the share of
rows where the true net value is negative is {{ n.harmful_share.zalo_message | pct(0) }} for `zalo_message`,
{{ n.harmful_share.price_promo | pct(0) }} for `price_promo`, {{ n.harmful_share.display_kit | pct(0) }} for
`display_kit` and {{ n.harmful_share.rep_visit | pct(0) }} for `rep_visit`. On the whole panel, the BAU rule's
true net gain over doing nothing is {{ n.true_bau_full | vnd }} VND per outlet-month and the ceiling (true best
arm everywhere, the *oracle*) is {{ n.true_oracle_full | vnd }} VND.

## 5. Definitions

Sections 3, 4, 6, 7 and 8 of this report follow the notebook: each heading "Part N" is notebook Part N, and
the line under each section heading gives the notebook cells (counted from 1, markdown cells included). Sections 1, 2, 5, 9, 10 and 12 have no notebook part (section 9 shows tables computed in Parts 8 and 11);
section 11 is Part 14.
Code excerpts use the notebook's short names: `h` is the case study helper module, `aum` the modelling
library, `it` an interpretation object, `rc` a robustness-check object and `ev` an evaluation object.
`ds_tr` and `ds_te` are the training and test outlets after the feature filter; `ds_null` is the training data with
an outcome no arm can change (A/A test); `oof` holds the out-of-fold predictions on the training outlets;
`frame_te` the predictions on the test outlets; `with_evidence()` keeps the rows where every arm has an estimated
propensity of at least 0.02. Cross-fitting uses K = 3 folds grouped by outlet (`h.N_FOLDS`).

Formulas use: `x` the features of a row, `y` its outcome, `arm` the arm it received, `k` an arm (0 to 4).

| Term | Meaning |
|------------|--------------------------------------|
| Arm | One of the five possible actions for an outlet-month, coded 0 to 4: `control` (0), `zalo_message` (1), `price_promo` (2), `display_kit` (3), `rep_visit` (4). Each outlet-month gets exactly one arm. |
| Control | Arm 0, no action. Every effect is measured against it. |
| Outcome, y | What the action should change: `cases_ordered`, the cases the outlet orders in the month (0 if it does not order). |
| Potential outcome, mu_k | The expected cases an outlet-month would order if it got arm k: `mu_k(x) = E[y | arm = k, x]`. Only one of the five is ever observed for a row. |
| Uplift, CATE, tau | The effect of arm k against control for a row with features x: `tau_k(x) = mu_k(x) - mu_0(x)`, in cases per outlet-month. CATE stands for conditional average treatment effect; "uplift" is the business word for the same thing. |
| Net value | What an arm is worth for a row in money: `60,000 x tau_k - cost_k`, in VND per outlet-month (60,000 VND gross margin per case). Control has net value 0. |
| Propensity, e_k | The probability that a row with features x received arm k in the data: `e_k(x) = P(arm = k | x)`. Known by design for trade-test rows, estimated by a model for the mix of test and BAU rows. |
| Overlap | Every arm has a non-negligible propensity for every kind of row. Where an arm was never given (for example `display_kit` in modern trade), there is no evidence of its effect there. |
| Propensity clip | A floor (0.01 here) applied to e_k inside the DR score so that a tiny propensity cannot blow up one row's weight. |
| Propensity floor | A policy rule (0.02 here): an arm whose estimated propensity for a row is below it is never chosen for that row. |
| Nuisance models | The models the effect estimate needs but that are not the effect itself: the outcome models mu_k and the propensity model e_k. |
| Cross-fitting | Every row's nuisance and effect predictions come from models fitted on other folds, so no row is scored by a model that saw it. Folds are grouped by outlet here. |
| DR score, gamma_k | The doubly robust estimate of the outcome a row would have had under arm k: `gamma_k = mu_k + 1[arm = k] (y - mu_k) / max(e_k, 0.01)`. Unbiased on average if either the outcome model or the propensity model is right. |
| DR learner (`dr`), T-learner (`t`), X-learner (`x`) | Three ways to estimate tau_k from the same nuisances. `dr` regresses `gamma_k - gamma_0` on x; `t` subtracts two outcome models; `x` imputes effects in each arm and blends them by propensity. |
| Policy | A rule that assigns one arm to every row, here from the predicted effects and the costs. |
| Policy value | The mean net value of a policy over rows, estimated with DR scores: `mean(60,000 x gamma[row, chosen arm] - cost[chosen arm])` (the total value, including the base margin). Reported as the gain over all-control, `mean(60,000 x (gamma[row, chosen] - gamma[row, control]) - cost[chosen])`, in VND per outlet-month. |
| BAU (business as usual) | The current rule (section 3) that assigned arms to the 30% of rows outside the trade test. It is also evaluated as a policy. |
| Oracle | The policy that gives every row its true best arm. Only computable on synthetic data; it is the ceiling. |
| Best single arm | The one arm that, given to every row, has the highest value. |
| Capacity bucket | A group of rows sharing a capacity limit: here one region in one month. |
| Share sweep | A search over the shares of rows given to each arm (`top_share` policy), valued with DR scores. |
| Uplift curve | For one arm, the net gain of giving it to the top x% of rows ranked by predicted effect, as x goes from 0 to 100%. |
| Qini | The area between the uplift curve and the straight line of random targeting. Positive means the ranking beats random. |
| Calibration | Whether predicted effect sizes match observed ones: rows predicted to gain 2 cases should gain about 2 cases. The calibration slope is 1 for a calibrated arm. |
| Feature importance | How much each feature moves the predicted effect of an arm (mean absolute TreeSHAP contribution, in cases). |
| Noise floor | The largest importance reached by shuffled copies of real features ("shadow" features, pure noise). A feature below it is not a reliable driver. |
| Decision importance | The share of rows whose chosen arm changes when one feature is shuffled across rows. |
| Paired difference | The difference in value between two policies on the same rows, with a bootstrap CI that resamples whole outlets. Much tighter than comparing two separate CIs. |
| CI | Confidence interval, here the 95% bootstrap interval. |
| MDE | Minimum detectable effect: the smallest true difference a pilot finds with 80% probability at the 5% level (two-sided). |
| PDP | Partial dependence: the mean predicted effect as one feature moves, the others kept as they are. |
| MAE | Mean absolute error. The "binned error" of Part 10 is the MAE between predicted and DR observed effect over bins of predicted effect. |
| AUUC | Area under the uplift curve. |
| Placebo test | Refit with the arm column shuffled, several times. Any gain the model then finds is noise; the real gain (same refit, real arms) must be above every shuffled one. |
| A/A test | Refit with an outcome the arm cannot affect (last month's volume). The model must not find a significant positive gain: the CI must not lie above zero. A negative gain is expected, since every arm then only costs money. |
| Referee | A separate DR learner fitted only on the evaluation rows to produce the DR scores, so a model is never graded with its own predictions. |
| BAU within the limits | The BAU rule cut down to the display and rep caps: where the rule exceeds a cap in a region-month, a random subset keeps the arm and the rest get control. The fair baseline for the capacity policy. |
| VND | Vietnamese dong, the currency of the simulation. 60,000 VND is the gross margin of one case. |
| LightGBM | The gradient-boosted tree library used for every model (outcome, propensity, effect). `num_leaves` and `min_child_samples` are its two main size settings. |
| TPE | Tree-structured Parzen estimator, the search method of the tuner (from the Optuna library): it proposes new settings near the ones that scored well. |
| TreeSHAP | An exact way to split a tree model's prediction into one contribution per feature. |
| ESS | Effective sample size of the DR weights for an arm: how many equally weighted rows the evidence is worth. Small ESS means weak overlap. |
| Regret | For one row, the true net value of the true best arm minus the true net value of the chosen arm. Only computable on synthetic data. |

## 6. Method

The method is described in full, with pseudocode, in `method/README.md` of the repository. This section lists
every choice made in the run, with the value used and the reason.

{{ nbref(3) }}

### Part 3. Train / test split

**Purpose.** Keep some outlets aside to measure the result honestly.

**Choice.** {{ (n.test_share) | pct(0) }} of outlets ({{ n.n_test_outlets | vnd }} outlets,
{{ n.n_test_rows | vnd }} rows) go to the test set with all their months; {{ n.n_train_outlets | vnd }} outlets
({{ n.n_train_rows | vnd }} rows) are used for training. The split is random by outlet (seed {{ n.split_seed }}), not
by time: the question is how well the model generalises to other outlets in the same months. Inside the
training set, the cross-fitting folds are also grouped by outlet (`group_col="outlet_id"`), so no outlet is
both in a model's training folds and in the rows it predicts.

`month_index` (0 to 5) is a *nuisance-only* feature: the outcome and propensity models use it to absorb
seasonality, but the effect models do not see it, because the effects must hold in future months.

```python
is_test = h.split_outlets(df)                         # 30% of outlets, seed 7
```

**Finding.** No outlet is in both sets.

{{ nbref(4) }}

### Part 4. Feature filter

**Purpose.** Remove features that cannot help before fitting anything: mostly missing, almost constant, or
almost a copy of another feature. Each removal comes with a reason. Association is measured with Cramér's V (`cramers_v`, 0 to 1) between two
categorical features, the absolute correlation between two numeric ones and the symmetric uncertainty
(`sym_uncertainty`, 0 to 1) between mixed ones; `target_assoc` is a feature's association with the outcome.

**Choice.** Missing above 80%, one value in more than 99% of rows, or association above 0.95 with another
feature (then the one less related to the outcome is dropped). `month_index` and `tet_month` are kept by
force (`KEEP` in the code): they are perfectly associated (Tet is month index 1) but play different roles.

```python
ff = aum.FeatureFilter(null_threshold=0.8, near_constant_threshold=0.99, correlation_threshold=0.95,
                       force_keep=KEEP).fit(ds_train)
```

{{ table("feature_filter", cols=["feature", "status", "reason"], size="small") }}

Where the reason shows two equal values joined by ">" (for example `target_assoc=0.001 > ... 0.001`), the values
differ beyond the 3 decimals printed; the comparison uses full precision.

**Finding.** The filter removed {{ n.removed_features | length }} features: {% for f, r in n.removed_features.items() %}`{{ f }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last else "" }}{% endfor %}.
Two of the three planted columns (the near-constant flag and the near copy)
are caught; the third, `noise_feature`, is discussed below. `region` is removed because `province` determines it; it stays in the
data for the capacity limits. `seating_capacity` stays although it is missing for about 60% of rows
(off-trade outlets have no seats; the gradient-boosted trees handle missing values). `noise_feature` passes:
no simple filter can tell noise from a weak real feature; the noise floor in Part 11 is the check for that.
{{ n.n_kept }} features remain ({{ n.n_kept - 1 }} for the effect models: `month_index` is nuisance-only).

{{ nbref(5) }}

### Part 5. Tuning: learner and LightGBM parameters

**Purpose.** Choose the effect learner and the model complexity on outlets the candidates were not fitted on.

**Choice.** Search space: learner `method` in {dr, t, x} (section 5), LightGBM `num_leaves` from 7 to 31 and
`min_child_samples` from 500 to 2,000, 300 trees at learning rate 0.05 with early stopping. Large minimum leaf size (`min_child_samples`) on
purpose: the effects (a few cases) are small next to the outcome noise (an outlet orders 0 to 300 cases).
Budget: {{ n.tuner_n_iter }} candidates proposed by TPE (a Bayesian search). Objective: the net value
(VND per outlet-month) of each candidate's best-arm decisions on 30% of the training outlets, measured with
doubly robust scores from a separate *referee* model fitted on those outlets only. Rule: among the candidates
not significantly worse than the best (paired bootstrap, 95%), the simplest wins.

```python
space = {"method": ["dr", "t", "x"], "num_leaves": ("int", 7, 31), "min_child_samples": ("int", 500, 2000),
...
```

{{ table("tuner_summary", cols=["method", "num_leaves", "min_child_samples", "score", "ci_low", "ci_high", "tied", "chosen"],
  query="status == 'ok'", rename={"score": "net value", "ci_low": "CI low", "ci_high": "CI high"}) }}

**How to read it.** `net value` is the mean net value per validation outlet-month in VND, including the
outlet's base margin (so all candidates are around 3 million); only differences between rows matter.
`tied` marks candidates not significantly worse than the best, by a *paired* test (both candidates scored
on the same validation rows). A candidate with a lower score can be tied while one with a higher score is not,
when its decisions differ less from the best's. The baselines (all-control, best single arm) are in the
notebook.

**Finding.** Chosen: `{{ n.method }}` with `num_leaves` {{ n.tuner_best_params.num_leaves }} and
`min_child_samples` {{ n.tuner_best_params.min_child_samples }}.
{{ tcount("tuner_summary", "tied == True") }} of the {{ n.tuner_n_iter }} candidates were not significantly worse
than the best one ({{ tcount("tuner_summary", "tied == True and method == 'dr'") }} `dr`,
{{ tcount("tuner_summary", "tied == True and method == 't'") }} `t`, {{ tcount("tuner_summary", "tied == True and method == 'x'") }} `x`);
among them the rule took the one with the fewest leaves. With that many candidates tied, the search cannot
single out a best one, which is why the rule prefers the simplest of the tied ones.

