### Part 6. Learners compared on shared nuisances

**Purpose.** A second look at the choice of learner, on all training outlets.

**How.** One fit of the nuisance models (outcome per arm, propensity) shared by the three learners, each
learner's best-arm policy valued out of fold against the BAU rule.

```python
cmp_model = aum.MultiArmLearner(methods=("dr", "t", "x"), n_folds=h.N_FOLDS, random_state=0, verbosity=0,
...
```

{{ table("methods_oof", rename={"oof_gain_vs_bau": "gain over BAU (VND)", "ci_low": "CI low", "ci_high": "CI high"}) }}

**Finding.** This second look does not agree with the tuner. Here `x` gains
{{ tval("methods_oof", "oof_gain_vs_bau", method="x") | vnd }} VND over BAU and `dr`
{{ tval("methods_oof", "oof_gain_vs_bau", method="dr") | vnd }} VND (separate intervals that overlap); the paired difference on the same rows, `x` minus `dr`, is
{{ n.x_minus_dr_oof.est_difference | vnd }} VND (CI {{ n.x_minus_dr_oof.ci_low | vnd }} to {{ n.x_minus_dr_oof.ci_high | vnd }}); `t` gains least here, and the tuner's lowest-scoring candidate is a `t`. The two looks use different rows (a 30% validation slice against all training outlets, out of fold) and a
different yardstick (a separate referee against the learner's own nuisances). Here the paired test favours `x`
significantly; in the tuner every `x` candidate was significantly worse than the best `dr`. So the two looks
contradict each other, and which learner is better is not settled.

**Decision.** Keep `dr`, the choice of the rule fixed before the run. Switching to `x` because it looks better
on a second, post hoc comparison would be picking the winner after seeing the scores. But `x` may be the better
learner; the next refit should compare the two on fresh data (for example the pilot's holdout). The test outlets
(Part 8) and the overfit report (Part 12) check the chosen model on data neither look used.

**Other settings.** Propensity clip in the doubly robust scores: 0.01. Propensity floor of the policy:
0.02 (an arm is never chosen where its estimated propensity is below 2%). Costs and value per case as in
section 2. Capacity: section 7, Part 7. Calibration: tested in Part 10, not applied (see there).

## 7. Results

Each part answers one question. Money is net margin in VND per outlet-month unless stated otherwise.

{{ nbref(7) }}

### Part 7. Policies: best arm, capacity, share sweep

**Purpose.** Turn the predicted effects into one arm per outlet-month, three ways.

**Choices.**

* `best`: the arm with the highest net value `{{ n.value_per_outcome | vnd }} x tau_k - cost_k`; control when
  no arm is positive. An arm whose estimated propensity is below 0.02 for a row is never chosen there: the data
  has no evidence of its effect for that kind of outlet.
* `capacity`: display kits and rep visits are capped per region and month (the caps of section 2, scaled to
  the test outlets: each region's cap times its share of outlets that are in the test set). Inside each region-month the arms are filled in an order chosen by the analyst with the truth of section 4 in view (the reverse order is checked on the
  training outlets below):
  `rep_visit` first, which goes to the rows where its net
  value is highest, up to the cap; then `display_kit` among the remaining rows; then `price_promo` and
  `zalo_message`, which have no cap. A row only gets an arm whose estimated net value is
  positive (`min_gain=0.0`), so a cap is a maximum, not a quota.
* `top_share`: the same filling, with a share of all rows per arm instead of a number per region-month. The
  shares come from a sweep over {{ n.sweep_n }} combinations on the training outlets.

```python
capacity = aum.MultiArmPolicy(value_per_outcome=VALUE, cost=COST, propensity_floor=h.PROPENSITY_FLOOR,
strategy="capacity", min_gain=0.0,     # never give an arm that loses money
capacity={4: cap_test[4], 3: cap_test[3], 2: unlimited, 1: unlimited},
capacity_by="region", period="M", date_col="month")
```

{{ table("allocation_channel") }}

**How to read it.** Each row is a channel under one policy; the five numbers are the share of its test
outlet-months (%) given each arm.

**Finding.** Without limits the model gives rep visits to {{ best["%_rep"] | f(1) }}% of rows (mostly bars and
restaurants) and display kits to {{ best["%_display"] | f(1) }}% (groceries and minimarts), close to the true best
mix of section 4. The caps cut rep visits to {{ capp["%_rep"] | f(1) }}% and display kits to {{ capp["%_display"] | f(1) }}%, and the
rows that lose their display kit or rep visit mostly get a price promo instead ({{ capp["%_promo"] | f(1) }}% of
rows), because the model predicts a promo pays for itself almost everywhere (Part 11 shows that this is too
optimistic). Modern trade gets no display kit or rep visit under any policy: the propensity floor keeps those
arms out. Every region looks alike, since the caps are the same share of outlets in every region (the
notebook shows the allocation by region in Part 7).

{{ table("sweep_top10", cols=["rank", "share_arm_1", "share_arm_2", "share_arm_3", "share_arm_4", "gain", "gain_ci_low", "gain_ci_high", "vs_best", "vs_best_ci_low", "vs_best_ci_high", "selected"],
  rename={"share_arm_1": "zalo", "share_arm_2": "promo", "share_arm_3": "display", "share_arm_4": "rep", "gain_ci_low": "CI low", "gain_ci_high": "CI high", "vs_best": "vs rank 1", "vs_best_ci_low": "vs 1 CI low", "vs_best_ci_high": "vs 1 CI high"}, size="small") }}

**How to read it.** Each row is one combination of shares (zalo, promo, display, rep). `gain` is its DR value
over the BAU rule on the training outlets, out of fold. The largest shares tried for display (6%) and rep (8%)
are the caps. Shares are fractions of rows (0.14 = 14%).
`vs rank 1` is the paired difference with rank 1 on the same rows, with its 95% CI; a combination is tied with
rank 1 when that CI includes zero. Because it is paired, a combination with a higher `gain` can be significantly
worse than rank 1 while one with a lower `gain` is tied.

**Finding.** Every combination is worth *less* than BAU (all gains are negative) because BAU breaks the caps:
it gets about twice as many display kits and rep visits. Among combinations that respect the caps, the
best ones use all of them. A share is a maximum: with `min_gain=0.0` an arm is only given where its
estimated net value is positive, so a zalo share of 20% or 40% gives the same 14% of rows (the duplicate
rows). The shares in the table are the shares actually given. The selection rule `simplest_tied` keeps the
combinations whose paired interval against rank 1 (`vs rank 1`) includes zero and, among them,
takes the one that treats the fewest rows: rank {{ n.sweep_selected.rank }} (zalo
{{ n.sweep_selected.share_arm_1 | pct(0) }} of rows, promo {{ n.sweep_selected.share_arm_2 | pct(0) }}, control
{{ n.sweep_selected.share_arm_0 | pct(0) }}). Its share parameters (zalo {{ n.top_share_shares["1"] | pct(0) }}, promo {{ n.top_share_shares["2"] | pct(0) }},
display {{ n.top_share_shares["3"] | pct(0) }}, rep {{ n.top_share_shares["4"] | pct(0) }}) are maxima: an arm is only given where its net value is
positive, so on the test outlets (Part 8) the shares actually given differ. Ranks 4, 5 and 10 treat even fewer rows but are significantly worse
than rank 1.

**Fill order, checked without the truth.** On the training outlets (out of fold, caps scaled to them), the
capacity policy with rep visits first minus the same policy with display kits first is
{{ n.fill_order_oof.est_difference | vnd }} VND per outlet-month (CI {{ n.fill_order_oof.ci_low | vnd }} to
{{ n.fill_order_oof.ci_high | vnd }}); {{ n.fill_order_oof.share_rows_changed | pct(1) }} of rows get a different arm.
The difference is not significant and its point estimate slightly favours display kits first: the order chosen
with the truth in view is not shown to matter.

