### Part 8. Policy value on the test outlets

**Purpose.** Measure what each policy is worth on the {{ n.n_test_outlets | vnd }} outlets the model never saw.

**How.** A separate DR learner, the *referee*, is fitted on the test rows only (cross-fitted by outlet). Its
out-of-fold models give a DR score per row and arm (section 5). The value of a policy is the mean over rows of
`{{ n.value_per_outcome | vnd }} x gamma[row, chosen arm] - cost[chosen arm]`. Decisions come from the model
trained on the training outlets, so the model never grades itself. Confidence intervals resample whole
outlets, 500 times.

```python
referee = aum.MultiArmDRLearner(n_folds=h.N_FOLDS, random_state=1, verbosity=0, group_col="outlet_id",
...
```

`model_capacity_promo_rule` is the capacity policy with one business rule added: no price promo where
`brand_share_est` is above 0.6 (section 9 explains why).

`bau_within_limits` is the BAU rule cut down to the caps: where the rule gives more display kits or rep
visits than the cap of a region-month, a random subset keeps the arm and the rest get `control`. It is the
fair baseline for the capacity policy.

{{ table("policy_table", cols=["policy", "est_gain", "ci_low", "ci_high", "true_gain", "gap_to_oracle", "monthly_total_mVND", "%_control", "%_zalo", "%_promo", "%_display", "%_rep"],
  rename={"est_gain": "est. gain", "ci_low": "CI low", "ci_high": "CI high", "true_gain": "true gain", "gap_to_oracle": "gap to oracle", "monthly_total_mVND": "mVND per month", "%_control": "% ctrl", "%_zalo": "% zalo", "%_promo": "% promo", "%_display": "% disp", "%_rep": "% rep"}, size="small") }}

**How to read it.** `est. gain` is the DR estimate of the net gain over giving every outlet `control`, in
VND per outlet-month, with its 95% CI. `true gain` is the same quantity from the true effects (synthetic data
only). `mVND per month` (million VND) scales the estimate to all {{ n.n_outlets | vnd }} outlets. The last five
columns are the arm mix in %.

**Finding.** Most estimates are below the truth, by up to about 62,000 VND (BAU within the limits is almost
exact), and far below it for `rep_visit_for_all` ({{ pt["rep_visit_for_all"].est_gain | vnd }} estimated against
{{ pt["rep_visit_for_all"].true_gain | vnd }} true) and `display_kit_for_all`. These two policies give the arm to
modern trade stores, which never got it in the data; the referee's scores there are extrapolations.
Policies that respect the propensity floor (the model's and BAU) are not affected. Among those, the estimated
order of the groups is the true order (model without limits, then BAU, then the capped policies), but the order
inside the capped group is not: the estimate puts `model_capacity` last, below `bau_within_limits`, while in truth
it is well above it. In truth `rep_visit_for_all`
(true {{ pt["rep_visit_for_all"].true_gain | vnd }}) is worth more than any capped policy, but it would need a rep
visit for every outlet, far beyond the caps.

{{ table("paired_differences", rename={"est_difference": "est. difference", "ci_low": "CI low", "ci_high": "CI high", "true_difference": "true difference"}) }}

**How to read it.** The difference in value between two policies on the same rows. Because both policies are
scored on the same outlets, this interval is narrower than the difference of two separate intervals.

**Finding.**

* Model without limits against BAU: {{ pb.est_difference | vnd }} VND (CI {{ pb.ci_low | vnd }} to
  {{ pb.ci_high | vnd }}), true {{ pb.true_difference | vnd }}.
* Model within the caps against BAU *within the caps*: {{ pcl.est_difference | vnd }} VND (CI {{ pcl.ci_low | vnd }}
  to {{ pcl.ci_high | vnd }}), true {{ pcl.true_difference | vnd }}. The truth favours the model, the estimate cannot
  separate them. The capped policy's estimate falls {{ (capp.true_gain - capp.est_gain) | vnd }} VND short of its true value,
  about as much as `model_best`'s ({{ (best.true_gain - best.est_gain) | vnd }}): both treat almost every row and share
  the shift in the control scores (FAQ, section 12). The estimate for BAU within the caps ({{ baucap["%_control"] | f(0) }}% control) is
  almost exact, so the shift does not cancel in this comparison; this is why the estimated difference misses. Against BAU without caps the capped model
  loses ({{ pc.est_difference | vnd }}, true {{ pc.true_difference | vnd }}), which only says that BAU uses
  display kits and rep visits the brand does not have.
* `model_top_share` respects the caps on average; against its fair baseline, BAU within the caps, it is at
  {{ n.paired["model_top_share - bau_within_limits"].est_difference | vnd }} VND (CI
  {{ n.paired["model_top_share - bau_within_limits"].ci_low | vnd }} to
  {{ n.paired["model_top_share - bau_within_limits"].ci_high | vnd }}; true
  {{ n.paired["model_top_share - bau_within_limits"].true_difference | vnd }}).
* The caps cost {{ pbc.est_difference | vnd }} VND per outlet-month (best minus capacity; true
  {{ pbc.true_difference | vnd }}). This is the price of the limits under this model, an upper bound on what more
  reps and display kits could earn.

{{ table("agreement", rename={"agreement_%": "model %", "bau_agreement_%": "BAU %"}) }}

**Finding.** The model picks the true best arm for {{ n.agreement["all rows"] | f(1) }}% of test rows, BAU for
{{ n.agreement_bau["all rows"] | f(1) }}%. The model is weakest in modern trade
({{ n.agreement.modern_trade | f(1) }}%), where only three arms are allowed and the choice between promo,
message and nothing depends on the promo effect, the effect it estimates worst.

**Decision.** The model picks the true best arm twice as often as BAU (table above), its rankings for display
kits and rep visits beat random targeting (Part 9), and the cost of the caps is significant. Without caps the gain
over BAU is positive but its interval includes zero; the overfit report (Part 12) points the same way but is not
an independent check. Within the caps the data does not settle it; section 9 sizes a pilot that can.

