### Part 10. Calibration

**Purpose.** The best-arm policy compares effect sizes across arms in VND, so the *size* of each predicted
effect matters, not only its order.

**How.** Rows are binned by predicted effect, and in each bin the mean predicted effect is compared with the DR
observed effect. The slope of observed on predicted is 1 for a well-calibrated arm. Only rows with evidence for
every arm are used (modern trade is left out). The slopes, the figure and the isotonic calibrator are fitted on
the training outlets, out of fold ({{ n.evidence_share_train | pct(0) }} of training rows have evidence); the error
before and after calibration and the effect on decisions are measured on the test outlets
({{ n.evidence_share_test | pct(0) }} of test rows).

```python
cal = aum.MultiArmCalibrator(method="isotonic").fit(with_evidence(oof))
```

{{ table("calibration_slope", cols=["arm", "mean_effect", "slope", "slope_ci_low", "slope_ci_high", "heterogeneity_detected"],
  rename={"mean_effect": "mean DR effect", "slope_ci_low": "CI low", "slope_ci_high": "CI high", "heterogeneity_detected": "differences detected"}) }}

{{ landscape_begin() }}

{{ figure("calibration", wide=True) }}

**How to read it.** A slope near 1 means the predicted differences between rows are about the right size; near
0, the predicted differences are not visible in the data; a CI that includes 0 means the data cannot confirm
that the arm's effect varies between rows.

{{ landscape_end() }}

**Finding.** For `display_kit` (slope {{ tval("calibration_slope", "slope", arm=3) | f(2) }}) and `rep_visit`
({{ tval("calibration_slope", "slope", arm=4) | f(2) }}) the data confirms that effects vary between rows, but
the predicted differences are larger than observed. For `zalo_message` and `price_promo` the data cannot confirm
any variation (CI includes 0) on these rows. On the test outlets the calibrator lowers the binned error for
display kits and rep visits but raises it for messages (from
{{ tval("calibration_error", "mae_raw", arm=1) | f(2) }} to {{ tval("calibration_error", "mae_calibrated", arm=1) | f(2) }}
cases) and slightly for promos ({{ tval("calibration_error", "mae_raw", arm=2) | f(2) }} to
{{ tval("calibration_error", "mae_calibrated", arm=2) | f(2) }}). The calibrator changes
{{ n.calibration_changed_share | pct(0) }} of the decisions and the value by {{ n.calibration_gain[0] | vnd }} VND
(CI {{ n.calibration_gain[1] | vnd }} to {{ n.calibration_gain[2] | vnd }}; true change
{{ n.calibration_true_gain | vnd }}).

**Decision.** Do not apply the calibration: it does not improve the decisions. The predicted differences between
rows are too wide for display kits and rep visits, and for promos the model's variation is not confirmed by
the data at all, although the true promo effect varies strongly with brand share (section 4). That is a model
weakness, not a calibration problem. (The overfit report of Part 12 shows slopes close to 1 for display kits
and rep visits, but on all rows, where modern trade's extrapolated effects dominate both sides of the fit;
it is not evidence of good calibration.)

