and {{ tval("promo_vs_share_observed", "actual", bin="[0.7, 0.99]") | f(1) }}; both intervals include zero, so these
bands only show the effect is small, not that it is negative), in line with the truth (section 4). The model's line falls only from
{{ tval("promo_vs_share_observed", "effect", bin="[0.03, 0.29]") | f(1) }} to
{{ tval("promo_vs_share_observed", "effect", bin="[0.7, 0.99]") | f(1) }} and stays above break-even
everywhere. The model has the direction right but shrinks the slope: `brand_share_est` is a noisy survey
estimate, and the promo effect (a few cases) is small next to the order noise.

{{ figure("explain_row", width="85%") }}

**How to read it.** One outlet-month (outlet {{ n.explain_row_id }}, {{ n.explain_row_month }}; the plot's "Row" label is its position in the sample): a large bar in the {{ n.explain_row_features.region }}, sales falling
(`vol_trend_3m` {{ n.explain_row_features.vol_trend_3m | f(2) }}), brand share
{{ n.explain_row_features.brand_share_est | f(2) }}. Each bar is a feature's contribution to the gap in net
value between the chosen arm and the runner-up. `average difference (all rows)` is the starting point of the
decomposition: the model's average gap between the two arms over the rows its trees were fitted on. It is
strongly negative because those rows include modern trade, where the model extrapolates rep visit effects far
below zero (Part 13); the feature bars then move this outlet from that average to its own gap.

**Finding.** It gets a rep visit rather than a price promo, by {{ tval("explain_row", "contribution", item="total: value gap") | vnd }} VND,
because it is a bar that orders often; its large volume pulls the other way (the promo pays more on big orders),
and so does `outlet_size` = large, although in the truth rep visits pay more in large outlets: a mismatch with the truth. The bar and
order-frequency contributions match the true structure (rep visits pay most in busy on-trade outlets).

**Decision.** Trust the model's choice between display kit, rep visit and the rest; do not trust its promo
effect sizes until `brand_share_est` is measured better or the promo is restricted by a rule (section 9).

{{ nbref(12) }}

### Part 12. Robustness checks

**Purpose.** Try to break the result. Most checks have a rule for passing; overlap and clip sensitivity are
judged as stated in the table, and the bootstrap is descriptive (no threshold was fixed in advance).

{{ need(6) }}
```python
rc = aum.MultiArmRobustnessChecker(model, ds_tr, method=METHOD, policy=best, n_boot=300, verbosity=0)
pl = rc.placebo_test(n_shuffles=19, max_rows=40_000)
aa = rc.aa_test(ds_null)
of = rc.overfit_report(ds_te)
```

| Check | Question | Result |
|----------|----------------|------------------------------------|
| Overlap | Does every arm have evidence for every kind of outlet? | Display kits and rep visits have propensities below the floor for {{ n.low_overlap_by_channel.modern_trade | pct(0) }} of modern trade rows and for no row of the other channels. The policy keeps them out there. |
| Clip sensitivity | Does the value depend on the propensity clip? | No: the same value for clips from 0.001 to 0.05. |
| Stability by month | Does the gain hold every month? (training outlets, out of fold) | The model loses to the best single arm in {{ n.model_loses_month }} of {{ n.n_months }} months. Passed. |
| Stability by channel | Does the gain hold in every channel? | The model loses to the best single arm in {{ n.model_loses_channel }} channels: bars and restaurants, where giving every outlet a rep visit is significantly better (see below). Failed. |
| Bootstrap | Do refits on subsamples choose the same arms? | `bootstrap_cate(n_bootstrap=5)` (the checker's `n_boot=300` sets the resamples of the CIs). {{ n.bootstrap_agreement | pct(0) }} of decisions are the same over {{ n.bootstrap_n }} refits on 80% of the rows. Descriptive: no threshold was fixed in advance, and 5 refits is few. |
| Placebo | With the arm column shuffled, does the gain disappear? | Gain over the best single arm, out of fold, on a refit with 40,000 training rows: real {{ n.placebo.actual_gain | vnd }} VND; the largest of {{ n.placebo.n_successful }} shuffled refits {{ n.placebo_null_max | vnd }} VND. p = {{ n.placebo.p_value }}, the smallest value 19 shuffles allow: the real gain beats every shuffle. Passed. |
| A/A | With an outcome the arm cannot change (last month's volume), does the model still find a gain? It must not find a significant *positive* gain. | Gain over all-control {{ n.aa.gain_vs_all_control | vnd }} VND (CI {{ n.aa.gain_vs_all_control_ci_low | vnd }} to {{ n.aa.gain_vs_all_control_ci_high | vnd }}) and over the best single arm {{ n.aa.gain_vs_best_single_arm | vnd }} (CI {{ n.aa.gain_vs_best_single_arm_ci_low | vnd }} to {{ n.aa.gain_vs_best_single_arm_ci_high | vnd }}): negative, because on such an outcome every arm only costs money. No positive gain, so {{ "passed" if n.aa.passes else "failed" }}. Against BAU it shows +{{ n.aa.gain_vs_bau_rule | vnd }} VND (CI {{ n.aa.gain_vs_bau_rule_ci_low | vnd }} to {{ n.aa.gain_vs_bau_rule_ci_high | vnd }}), because the A/A model spends little on arms while BAU spends about {{ spend("bau_rule") | float | vnd }} VND per outlet-month: BAU is not a valid baseline for this null check. `model_best` (about {{ spend("model_best") | float | vnd }}), `model_capacity` ({{ spend("model_capacity") | float | vnd }}) and the promo-rule policy ({{ spend("model_capacity_promo_rule") | float | vnd }}) spend more than BAU as written, so the gain of `model_best` over BAU is not a spending effect. The capped policies are compared with `bau_within_limits`, which spends about {{ spend("bau_within_limits") | float | vnd }}: all of them, `model_top_share` ({{ spend("model_top_share") | float | vnd }}) included, spend more. |
| Overfit | Are in-sample numbers far above the test numbers? | In sample is inflated; out of fold and test are not. See the table below. |

{{ table("stability_channel", cols=["group", "n", "gain_vs_best_single_arm", "gain_vs_best_single_arm_ci_low", "gain_vs_best_single_arm_ci_high", "gain_vs_bau_rule", "gain_vs_bau_rule_ci_low", "gain_vs_bau_rule_ci_high", "model_loses"],
  rename={"group": "channel", "gain_vs_best_single_arm": "vs best single arm", "gain_vs_best_single_arm_ci_low": "CI low", "gain_vs_best_single_arm_ci_high": "CI high",
          "gain_vs_bau_rule": "vs BAU", "gain_vs_bau_rule_ci_low": "CI low ", "gain_vs_bau_rule_ci_high": "CI high ", "model_loses": "model loses"}, size="small") }}

**How to read it.** Training outlets, out of fold ({{ n.n_train_rows | vnd }} rows in all). `vs best single arm`
compares the model with the one arm that is best for the channel as a whole, given to every outlet of it.

**Finding.** In bars and restaurants the model loses to "rep visit for every outlet" in that channel (bars
{{ tval("stability_channel", "gain_vs_best_single_arm", group="on_trade_bar") | vnd }} VND, restaurants
{{ tval("stability_channel", "gain_vs_best_single_arm", group="on_trade_restaurant") | vnd }} VND, both intervals
below zero),
because it gives a promo to many of them instead (on the test outlets
{{ tval("allocation_channel", "price_promo", policy="best", channel="on_trade_bar") | f(0) }}% of bar and
{{ tval("allocation_channel", "price_promo", policy="best", channel="on_trade_restaurant") | f(0) }}% of restaurant
rows, Part 7): the promo weakness again. In modern trade the
comparisons are too noisy to judge.

{{ table("overfit", cols=["metric", "in_sample", "out_of_fold", "test", "test_ci_low", "test_ci_high"], rows=[0, 1, 2, 3, 8, 10],
  rename={"in_sample": "in sample", "out_of_fold": "out of fold", "test_ci_low": "test CI low", "test_ci_high": "test CI high"}, size="small") }}

**How to read it.** The same number computed three ways: on the rows the model was fitted on (in sample), out
of fold on the training outlets, and on the test outlets. In-sample numbers are always too good; out of fold and
test should agree within their intervals (they use different outlets).

**Finding.** In sample is far too optimistic (gain over BAU {{ tval("overfit", "in_sample", metric="gain_vs_bau_rule") | vnd }}
VND); out of fold ({{ tval("overfit", "out_of_fold", metric="gain_vs_bau_rule") | vnd }}) and on the test outlets
({{ tval("overfit", "test", metric="gain_vs_bau_rule") | vnd }}, CI {{ tval("overfit", "test_ci_low", metric="gain_vs_bau_rule") | vnd }}
to {{ tval("overfit", "test_ci_high", metric="gain_vs_bau_rule") | vnd }}) the numbers are not inflated. The two differ by about
{{ (tval("overfit", "test", metric="gain_vs_bau_rule") - tval("overfit", "out_of_fold", metric="gain_vs_bau_rule")) | r3 | vnd }} VND,
on different outlets; their intervals overlap widely (out of fold {{ tval("methods_oof", "ci_low", method="dr") | vnd }} to {{ tval("methods_oof", "ci_high", method="dr") | vnd }}, Table 10). The test
number is scored with the training model's own outcome models, so it is not independent of the model; the
referee's estimate of Part 8 ({{ pb.est_difference | vnd }}, CI includes zero) is the one to rely on. The
library's automatic labels for these rows ("test gain proven") are left out for this reason.

**Clip sensitivity.** The value is identical for every clip because no propensity of an arm actually given to a
row is below 0.05: the small propensities belong to arms that modern trade stores never got, and those never
enter a DR correction term.

**Decision.** The gain is not noise (placebo), the method does not invent gains where none exist (A/A, against
all-control and the best single arm), the held-out numbers are not inflated (only the in-sample ones are), and the
value does not depend on the clip. The weak spots are the promo effect and the channels where one arm
is best almost everywhere.

