### Part 13. Model against the truth

**Purpose.** Grade the predicted effects on the test outlets against the true effects. Only possible because the
data is synthetic.

```python
mvt = h.model_vs_truth(frame_te, truth_te, df_te, meta)
```

{{ table("model_vs_truth", query="arm in ['display_kit', 'rep_visit', 'price_promo']", rename={"mean_predicted": "mean predicted", "mean_true": "mean true"}, size="small") }}

**How to read it.** Per arm and channel: mean predicted and mean true effect (cases per outlet-month), `bias` =
predicted minus true, `corr` = correlation between predicted and true effect across rows. The
`zalo_message` rows are in the notebook; its true effect is about half a case everywhere and the model
cannot rank it (Part 9).

{{ landscape_begin() }}

{{ figure("pred_vs_true", wide=True) }}

**How to read it.** One dot per test row (4,000 random rows); the dashed line is a perfect model. Orange dots
are modern trade.

{{ landscape_end() }}

**What the model got right.**

* Display kits in groceries and minimarts: almost no bias and a correlation of
  {{ tval("model_vs_truth", "corr", arm="display_kit", channel="off_trade_grocery") | f(2) }} and
  {{ tval("model_vs_truth", "corr", arm="display_kit", channel="off_trade_minimart") | f(2) }} with the true effect.
* Rep visits outside modern trade: bias below half a case in every channel, correlation
  {{ tval("model_vs_truth", "corr", arm="rep_visit", channel="off_trade_grocery") | f(2) }} in groceries.
* The average promo effect: {{ n.model_vs_truth.price_promo.mean_predicted | f(2) }} predicted against
  {{ n.model_vs_truth.price_promo.mean_true | f(2) }} true.

**What it got wrong, and why.**

* *Modern trade, display kits and rep visits*: predicted {{ n.modern_trade_pred.display_kit | f(0) }} and
  {{ n.modern_trade_pred.rep_visit | f(0) }} cases, against about +2 and +1 true. These stores never got either
  arm, so the model extrapolates. This is why the averages over all rows look badly biased
  ({{ n.model_vs_truth.display_kit.bias | f(1) }} and {{ n.model_vs_truth.rep_visit.bias | f(1) }} cases). It
  does not change any decision, because the propensity floor never lets the policy use these predictions.
* *Promo effect across outlets*: correlation only {{ n.model_vs_truth.price_promo.corr | f(2) }}. The model
  shrinks the dependence on brand share (Part 11), so it cannot tell where the promo loses money.
* *Rep visits in bars and restaurants*: right on average, but the correlation is only
  {{ tval("model_vs_truth", "corr", arm="rep_visit", channel="on_trade_bar") | f(2) }} in bars: it misses part of
  the size x trend interaction.

{{ table("misses", rename={"true_best": "true best", "mean_regret_vnd": "mean regret (VND)"}) }}

**How to read it.** The most common wrong choices of the best-arm policy: the true best arm, the arm chosen, how
many test rows, and the mean *regret* (true net value lost against the true best arm).

**Finding.** The model chooses a different arm than the truth for {{ n.share_wrong | pct(0) }} of test rows,
and the mean regret over all rows is {{ n.mean_regret | vnd }} VND per outlet-month. Per wrong choice the regret
has quartiles {{ n.regret_miss_quantiles["0.25"] | vnd }}, {{ n.regret_miss_quantiles["0.5"] | vnd }} and
{{ n.regret_miss_quantiles["0.75"] | vnd }} VND. So the misses are mostly not near-ties: half of them cost more than
{{ n.regret_miss_quantiles["0.5"] | vnd }} VND per outlet-month. The mean over all rows is lower because
{{ (1 - n.share_wrong) | pct(0) }} of rows have no miss. The most frequent miss is a promo where a rep visit was best, followed by the
reverse. They come from the promo effect being too flat and the rep effect being poorly ranked in bars.

## 9. Recommendation and rollout

**What to test.** The capacity policy (Part 7): each month, per region, rep visits and display kits go to the
outlets where the model expects the largest net value, up to the caps; the remaining outlets get a price promo or
a message only where it is expected to pay.

**Why a pilot and not a switch.** On the test outlets the capacity policy is estimated at
{{ pcl.est_difference | vnd }} VND per outlet-month against BAU within the same caps (CI {{ pcl.ci_low | vnd }} to
{{ pcl.ci_high | vnd }}). The truth says {{ pcl.true_difference | vnd }} VND, about
{{ (pcl.true_difference * n.n_outlets) | mvnd }} million VND per month over {{ n.n_outlets | vnd }} outlets, but a real
project would only see the estimate, and it does not separate the two policies. A pilot reduces this
uncertainty but, at this scale, does not remove it (see the power table below).

**One business rule on top.** No price promo where `brand_share_est` is above 0.6 (rows with a missing share are
not affected). Why: the DR observed promo effect is small in the two bands above 0.62, with point estimates below
zero (Part 11), while the model still predicts that the promo pays there. The cutoff 0.6 is the analyst's choice,
made with both those observed bands and the truth of section 4 in view, so the policy with the rule is
illustrative rather than a tested design; a real project would only have the bands.
The fill order of the capacity policy (rep visits first, then display kits) is also an analyst's choice; Part 7
compares it with display kits first on the training outlets (not significant). It was set with the true values of section 4 in view (rep visits are the most
valuable arm in on-trade), which a real project would not have; a real project would set it from the estimated
net values. The rule was valued on the test
outlets (`model_capacity_promo_rule`, Part 8): {{ capg.est_gain | vnd }} VND per outlet-month (true
{{ capg.true_gain | vnd }}), against {{ capp.est_gain | vnd }} (true {{ capp.true_gain | vnd }}) without it; the
difference is {{ pgc.est_difference | vnd }} VND (CI {{ pgc.ci_low | vnd }} to {{ pgc.ci_high | vnd }}; true
{{ pgc.true_difference | vnd }}). Against BAU within the caps the guarded policy is at {{ pgl.est_difference | vnd }}
VND (CI {{ pgl.ci_low | vnd }} to {{ pgl.ci_high | vnd }}; true {{ pgl.true_difference | vnd }}). The rule improves
the policy in truth and in the estimate, but neither difference is significant on the test outlets, so it is a
low-risk guard rather than a proven gain. It is meant for the pilot, to drop once a refit gets the slope right.

The bands that suggested the cutoff come from test rows, and the rule was then valued on the same test outlets,
so the rule's test estimates above are optimistic: the cutoff was chosen where it looked best on those rows.
The notebook's Part 11 therefore also computes the observed promo effect by band on the training outlets (out-of-fold DR scores, rows
with evidence for every arm), which did not value the policy:

{{ table("promo_cutoff_train", rename={"observed_effect": "observed effect (cases)", "ci_low": "CI low", "ci_high": "CI high"}) }}

**Finding.** On the training outlets the promo pays clearly below a share of 0.45 (about 5 cases), is at
break-even between 0.45 and 0.6 ({{ tval("promo_cutoff_train", "observed_effect", band="(0.45, 0.6]") | f(1) }} cases),
and is at or below break-even above 0.6 ({{ tval("promo_cutoff_train", "observed_effect", band="(0.6, 0.7]") | f(1) }}
and {{ tval("promo_cutoff_train", "observed_effect", band="(0.7, 1.0]") | f(1) }} cases, both intervals include 2).
So the training outlets support a cutoff near 0.6, but only weakly: they do not show that the promo loses money
above it, only that it does not clearly pay.

**How large a difference can a pilot detect?**

{{ table("pilot_power", rename={"outlets_per_side": "outlets per side", "sd_per_outlet": "sd per outlet", "se_difference": "SE of difference", "mde_80_power": "MDE (80% power)", "r2_adjustment": "R2 of adjustment"}) }}

**How to read it.** `sd per outlet` is the spread over outlets of an outlet's mean monthly gross margin over the pilot
window (VND), from the panel itself (arm costs are fixed per arm, so they add no noise). `raw` compares the two sides' means; `adjusted` first removes what the
pre-pilot features (`vol_avg_3m`, `order_frequency_3m`, `n_coolers`, channel) explain, which is the R2 column.
`MDE = 2.8 x SE`, the usual 80% power and 5% two-sided level.

**Finding.** A raw comparison over three months detects only about
{{ tval("pilot_power", "mde_80_power", months=3, analysis="raw") | vnd }} VND per outlet-month. The adjusted
analysis halves that ({{ tval("pilot_power", "mde_80_power", months=3, analysis="adjusted") | vnd }}), and six
months help little ({{ tval("pilot_power", "mde_80_power", months=6, analysis="adjusted") | vnd }}), because an
outlet's level persists from month to month. The true gap within the caps is {{ pcl.true_difference | vnd }} VND for the capacity policy
and {{ pgl.true_difference | vnd }} VND for the policy with the promo rule, which the pilot would test: both below
every MDE. With 3 months and the adjusted analysis (SE {{ se3 | vnd }}), the power is about
{{ (100 * phi(pcl.true_difference / se3 - 1.96)) | round | int }}% at {{ pcl.true_difference | vnd }} and about
{{ (100 * phi(pgl.true_difference / se3 - 1.96)) | round | int }}% at {{ pgl.true_difference | vnd }}. Reaching 80% power
at about 50,000 VND would need about
{{ (side_n * ((tval("pilot_power", "mde_80_power", months=3, analysis="adjusted") / 50000) ** 2)) | r3 | vnd }} outlets
per side, more than the outlet base. It is still worth running: it caps the risk (stop if the model is significantly worse), it removes the dependence on the BAU
history, and its holdout gives the clean data a better model needs. Use the adjusted analysis.

**How to roll out.**

1. *Split the pilot at random.* Half of the outlets (chosen at random, whole outlets, within each region so the
   caps split evenly) follow the model within the caps, the other half BAU within the caps: about
   {{ tval("pilot_power", "outlets_per_side", months=3, analysis="raw") | vnd }} outlets per side after the holdout.
   The two sides are different outlets, so the comparison is less precise than the paired one of Part 8.
   {{ ref("tab:pilot_power") }} gives the smallest true difference the pilot detects with 80% probability.
2. *Keep a random holdout.* About 10% of outlets, drawn at random once for the whole pilot and kept out of the
   two sides, get a random arm every month among the arms allowed for them (the same design as the trade test). This keeps the propensities known, lets the effects be
   re-estimated, and gives an honest measurement of the policy against BAU each month.
3. *Look after months 1 and 2, decide after month 3*, with the adjusted analysis of {{ ref("tab:pilot_power") }}:
   the two sides are randomised, so their observed net margin per outlet is compared directly, after removing
   what the pre-pilot features explain (no DR scores needed). The power figures above are for this analysis. At the two interim looks,
   stop only if the 99.9% interval of the difference lies entirely below zero (a Haybittle-Peto rule: so strict a
   level keeps the chance of a false stop low and leaves the final 5% analysis, and the MDE of
   {{ ref("tab:pilot_power") }}, almost unchanged). The final decision uses the 95% interval and a margin fixed in
   advance by the business.
4. *Log for every outlet-month:* the features at decision time, the predicted effect and net value of every
   arm, the arm chosen, whether the outlet was in the holdout, the capacity used, and the outcome. Without the
   predictions and the holdout flag the next model cannot be evaluated.
5. *Refit every quarter*, or earlier if the costs, the caps or the mix of outlets change, and rerun the checks
   of Part 12 before each switch.
6. *Weigh more reps and display kits.* The caps cost {{ pbc.est_difference | vnd }} VND per outlet-month (Part 8),
   CI {{ pbc.ci_low | vnd }} to {{ pbc.ci_high | vnd }}: an estimated upper bound of what more capacity could earn under this model.

## 10. Limitations and threats to validity

* **Synthetic data.** The outlets, effects, costs and caps were simulated (section 4). The case study shows how
  the method behaves when the truth is known; it says nothing about the size of effects in a real market. With
  real data there is no truth, and sections 4 and 8 could not be written.
* **A simple effect structure.** Each arm's effect depends on a few features through smooth formulas, and an
  arm's direct effect lasts only the month it is given (it changes later months only through the history features). Real effects can be weaker and noisier, and a display kit stays in the
  outlet for more than one month.
* **Fixed promo cost.** The promo costs a fixed {{ n.cost["2"] | vnd }} VND per outlet-month. A real "buy 10, get
  1 free" costs more where more is ordered, which would make the promo even less attractive in large, loyal
  outlets.
* **Simple caps.** One number per region and month. Real rep capacity depends on routes and travel time.
* **No evidence where an arm was never given.** Modern trade stores never got display kits or rep visits. The
  policy does not use the model's effects there, but the question "would a rep visit help a supermarket?" stays
  open until it is tested.
* **Noisy inputs.** `brand_share_est` is a noisy survey estimate, missing for 8% of rows. The model cannot be
  better than its inputs, which is the main reason for its promo weakness.
* **Choice of learner.** The tuner and the second comparison (Part 6) rank `dr` and `x` differently, each
  significantly, so which learner is better is not settled; the next refit should compare them on fresh data.
* **One data draw, one split.** The intervals, the bootstrap and the placebo test measure part of the
  uncertainty, not all of it.
* **No interference.** A promo in one outlet could take volume from the outlet next door; the method assumes
  each outlet's orders depend only on its own arm.
* **Single machine training.** Training ran on one laptop ({{ n.n_train_rows | vnd }} rows); scoring at larger
  scale (for example with PySpark) was not tested here.

