## 12. FAQ

**Why not just compare outlets that got an action with outlets that did not?**
Because the BAU rule chose who got what. It gave display kits to outlets with coolers and rep visits to large
bars, which order differently anyway. Section 3 shows the raw comparison says a display kit *loses* cases when
it truly adds them.

**Why not run an A/B test for each action and stop there?**
An A/B test gives the average effect of an action. The decision here is per outlet: the same rep visit gains
9 cases in a bar and 1 in a supermarket in the simulation's truth (section 4; real data has no rep visits in
supermarkets). The trade test rows are the A/B test; the model uses them, with the
BAU rows, to estimate who gains what.

**What is the money result, in one line?**
Without caps, the model earns {{ pb.est_difference | vnd }} VND per outlet-month more than BAU (true
{{ pb.true_difference | vnd }}). Within the caps, the truth says {{ pcl.true_difference | vnd }} VND more than BAU within
the same caps, but the estimate ({{ pcl.est_difference | vnd }}, CI {{ pcl.ci_low | vnd }} to {{ pcl.ci_high | vnd }})
cannot confirm it; hence the pilot of section 9.

**Why is the capped model estimated below BAU in {{ ref("tab:paired_differences") }}?**
The estimate is not significant (the CI includes zero), but it is also below in truth
({{ n.paired["model_capacity - bau_rule"].true_difference | vnd }} VND), because BAU breaks the caps: it uses about twice as many display kits and rep visits as the brand has. The fair
comparison is with BAU cut down to the same caps. There the truth favours the model and the estimate is
inconclusive.

**How do you know the model's estimate of the gain is honest?**
The model is trained on {{ n.n_train_outlets | vnd }} outlets and graded on {{ n.n_test_outlets | vnd }} others, by
a separate referee model fitted only on those outlets. The placebo test shows a model fitted on shuffled arms
finds almost nothing, and on this synthetic data the true gains are known and fall inside the intervals for the
policies that respect the propensity floor (not for `display_kit_for_all` and `rep_visit_for_all`, see Part 8).

**Why are the estimated gains lower than the true gains?**
Every gain is measured against the same estimate of "everyone gets control". An error in the control scores
only matters for the rows a policy does not give control, so it shifts mostly the gains of policies that treat almost every row: `model_best` (3% control) is about
62,000 VND low, while `bau_within_limits` (61% control) is almost exact. The shift is not simply proportional
to the share treated (`bau_rule` and `model_top_share` are about 38,000 and 34,000 VND low). Two policies that treat almost the same rows share this error, which is why best minus capacity
is almost exact. The capped policy treats almost every row and BAU within the caps does not, so the shift does not
cancel in their comparison, which is why it misses (Part 8). The true
gains of the model and BAU policies are all inside their intervals.

**Which outlets get a rep visit?**
Bars and restaurants that order often, especially when sales are falling (Part 11). Display kits go to groceries
and minimarts with coolers.

**Why does the model choose so many price promos within the caps?**
Once the caps on display kits and rep visits are reached, the promo is the arm left with the largest predicted
net value. The model predicts a promo pays almost everywhere, which is too optimistic where the brand share is
high. Hence the extra rule in section 9.

**What happens in supermarkets (modern trade)?**
Display kits and rep visits were never used there, so there is no evidence of their effect. The policy chooses
only between nothing, a message and a promo there.

**Is `noise_feature` a problem?**
It passes the noise floor and switches {{ tval("decision_importance", "share_switched", feature="noise_feature") | pct(1) }} of
decisions, but loses no significant value (Part 11). The floor is a screen; decision
importance is the stronger check. A real project would remove it on business grounds.

**Why not apply the calibration?**
It changed {{ n.calibration_changed_share | pct(0) }} of the decisions and did not improve their value on the test
outlets (Part 10).

**How often should the model be refitted?**
Every quarter, or when costs, caps or the outlet base change, with the random holdout of section 9 kept running
so each refit has fresh unbiased data.

**Can I re-run the notebook?**
Not from the repository: the modelling library is private. The notebook is published with every output; the
data generator, its tests and the PDF build run on their own (section 11).
